@page "/agent-steps"

<CatHead Title="Steps and approvals">
    What an agent thought, which tools it ran, what it means to do next, and what it asks permission
    for. The person stays in charge: every step can be opened and read, and nothing that writes happens
    before somebody says yes. All of it is markup the app writes, in the app's words, with no script.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentSteps.Reasoning" Title="Reasoning" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>details.reasoning</code> folds away the thinking behind an answer. It is a native
        <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, so it opens from the keyboard and with scripting off;
        <code>.reasoning-chevron</code> turns as it opens, and <code>.reasoning-body</code> hangs the text
        off a rule under it. The summary says how long the agent thought, which is what most readers want
        to know without opening it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentSteps.Steps" Title="Steps" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>ol.agent-steps</code> is the tool calls behind an answer, in order. Each <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>
        carries <code>data-state</code> &mdash; <code>pending</code>, <code>running</code>,
        <code>done</code> or <code>failed</code>, the same words as <a href="/collections"><code>.steps</code></a>
        &mdash; an <code>.agent-step-icon</code>, and a <code>details.agent-step</code> that opens on what
        the step sent and what came back. <code>.agent-step-meta</code> is the count and the time, on the
        end. A running step's marker is a small <a href="/orb">orb</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Show the call, not a description of it.</strong> The <code>.code-block</code> inside is
        what lets a reader check the agent's work; a failed step keeps the error in its own words.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentSteps.Plan" Title="Plan" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>ul.agent-plan</code> is what the agent means to do and how far it got: <code>data-state</code>
        on each item is <code>done</code>, <code>current</code> or <code>skipped</code>, or nothing for a
        step still to come. Mark the current one <code>aria-current="step"</code> too. A
        <a href="/progress"><code>.progress</code></a> above it gives the same count at a glance.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentSteps.Approval" Title="Asking before it acts" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.agent-approval</code> on a <a href="/card"><code>.card</code></a> is the agent asking
        permission. <code>.agent-approval-change</code> is the change itself &mdash; what, from, to &mdash;
        and chips under it say what kind of change it is. Approve is <code>.btn-go</code>, because it
        lets the action out; taking control back is <code>.btn-warn</code>; deny is quiet. The library's
        own meanings, so an approval reads the same as every other outward action in the app.
    </p>
    <p>
        Once the reader answers, <code>data-state="approved"</code> or <code>"denied"</code> swaps
        <code>.agent-approval-actions</code> for <code>.agent-approval-result</code>, which names who
        answered and when. Keep an undo there while the change can still be taken back.
    </p>
</CatExample>
